<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bermudo III of León</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1017–<a href="/years/1037">1037</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bermudo III was king of León from 1028 until 1037, the last of the male line descended from the Astur-Leonese kings. After losing ground to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a>, he went to war with Ferdinand, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and husband of his sister Sancha. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tamaron">Battle of Tamarón</a> in 1037, and Ferdinand became king of León.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bermudo-iii-of-leon.jpg" alt="Bermudo III of León" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jose Luis Lamigueiro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bermudo_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1037 Ferdinand I defeated and killed <b>Bermudo III of León</b> at the Battle of Tamarón and was crowned king of León, and he is traditionally counted as the first king of Castile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…effectively autonomous in the mid-tenth century, and in 1037 Ferdinand I, count of Castile and son of Sancho III of Navarre, killed <b>Bermudo III</b>, the last king of the old Leonese line, at the Battle of Tamarón and took the Leonese throne by right of his wife Sancha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
